     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Hello Phil,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Is there a difference between the bifactor IRT model\r\nand testlet response theory (TRT)? I always thought TRT is a bifactor model with a general factor and some specific factors (each testlet is considered a specific factor). However, in some papers I have seen that researchers have compared a\r\nbifactor model and a testlet model for a dataset. This implies that they are\r\ndifferent models. Can you please explain? Thanks for your help.</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Best</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Afshin</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\"> </span></p><div><br></div>-- <br><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Afshin Khosravi, PhD TEFL, MA TEFL<div><div><div><div><br></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>\r\n"
